/*This is a classic Sliding Window problem disguised as a swapping problem.

Step 1: Understand the goal

Input:

2 1 5 6 3 4 7
K = 3

Elements ≤ K are:

2 1 3

There are 3 good elements.

We want all these good elements together.

For example:

[2 1 3] 5 6 4 7

or

5 [2 1 3] 6 4 7

etc.

Step 2: Count good elements

Good element:

arr[i] <= K

Count them:

2,1,3

So:

good = 3

This means our final group must occupy a window of size 3.

Step 3: Find bad elements inside every window

Bad element:

arr[i] > K

Consider windows of size 3.

Window 1
[2 1 5]

Bad elements:

5

Count = 1

Window 2
[1 5 6]

Bad elements:

5,6

Count = 2

Window 3
[5 6 3]

Bad elements:

5,6

Count = 2

Window 4
[6 3 4]

Bad elements:

6,4

Count = 2

Window 5
[3 4 7]

Bad elements:

4,7

Count = 2

Minimum bad count:

1

Answer:

1
Why does "minimum bad elements" = "minimum swaps"?

Suppose window:

[2 1 5]

Good elements needed:

2 1 3

Inside window, only one bad element exists:

5

Outside window, one good element exists:

3

Swap:

5 ↔ 3

Now:

[2 1 3]

Only 1 swap required.

Therefore:

Minimum swaps = Minimum bad elements
in any window of size good.
Concept Used
Sliding Window
Count good elements (<= K)
Window size = good count
Count bad elements in first window
Slide the window
Update bad count efficiently
Keep minimum bad count
Complexity
Time  : O(n)
Space : O(1)*/



import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] arr = new int[n];

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }

        int k = sc.nextInt();

        // Count elements <= k (good elements)
        int good = 0;
        for(int x : arr) {
            if(x <= k)
                good++;
        }

        // Count bad elements (> k) in first window
        int bad = 0;
        for(int i = 0; i < good; i++) {
            if(arr[i] > k)
                bad++;
        }

        int ans = bad;

        // Sliding Window
        for(int i = good; i < n; i++) {

            // Remove element leaving window
            if(arr[i - good] > k)
                bad--;

            // Add element entering window
            if(arr[i] > k)
                bad++;

            ans = Math.min(ans, bad);
        }

        System.out.println(ans);
    }
}